[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject))
and marked known: one issue, one fix queued. The desk's own table says otherwise. Its reports are
three problems, and the queued parser fix closes only one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket and its own fix.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

This is the desk's own view, as exported on 4 October: one report per id (the export lists some
reports twice), each placed by its tenant and channel in `causes.ts`, never by subject or intake tag.
The table marks the `hint` row "no ticket of its own": it has one now, #2.

## One ticket per problem

<SplitTable ticket={4471} />

The reports the desk read as a one-day move stay in the `parser` row, where the desk files them by
tenant, so #1 counts them too. They also have a ticket of their own, #3.

### `parser`: dates read month first (#1)

The shared parser reads every date month first. Our tenants all write the day first, so a booking for
7 April comes out as 4 July. It reaches every tenant, through the app, by email and through the
booking-v2 form. This is the bug #4471 was opened for, and the fix that is queued.

### `hint`: booking-v3 asks for the month first (#2)

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. On a day-first tenant, people type the month
first as the hint tells them, and the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. The parser fix
does not reach it: only the form does, on booking-v3 tenants.

### One-day move: the booking lands a day off (#3)

The desk read these reports as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`). They are not a flip: the date moves
by one day ("Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday"), and the same meeting falls on different days in
Lisbon and Toronto. A day/month swap cannot do that.

**Not confirmed.** The export does not say why. A time-zone or day-boundary conversion of the invite
would fit; check how invite dates move between the tenant's and the recipient's time zone.

## How to read a report

- Place a report by its tenant's locale and booking form, not by its subject. The same subjects come
  in for every problem above.
- Do not use the intake tag. It follows the reporter's browser, not the cause.

